  <section class="lp-section lp-paper" id="hubspot-pricing"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">02 / Check your current bill</p><h2>Where HubSpot<br>gets expensive.</h2><p>Free and Starter can be good value. The bigger jump comes when your team needs sales sequences and stronger automation.</p></div>
    <div class="hs-plan-grid">
      <div><h3>Free CRM</h3><strong>$0</strong><span>Up to 2 users</span><p>Up to 1,000 contacts for basic contact and deal management.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Sales Starter</h3><strong>$20</strong><span>Standard / Core Seat / month</span><p>Limited sales automation. New-customer offers start lower.</p></div>
      <div class="hs-pro"><h3>Professional</h3><strong>$90</strong><span>/ Sales Seat / month, paid annually</span><p>Sales sequences and broader automation. Required onboarding adds $1,500.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Enterprise</h3><strong>$150</strong><span>/ Sales Seat / month, billed annually</span><p>Advanced controls. Required onboarding adds $3,500.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hs-two"><div><h3>The seat price is only part of it.</h3><p>Five Professional Sales Seats cost <strong>$6,900 in year one</strong>: $5,400 in annual licences plus $1,500 onboarding. Paying $100 per seat monthly raises that figure to $7,500 and still requires an annual commitment.</p><p>Professional includes 3,000 calling minutes per account each month, shared across users. An extra 1,000 minutes costs $50 monthly. Check the calling countries and limits your campaign needs.</p></div><div><h3>Check which Hub you are replacing.</h3><p>Marketing Hub contact charges are separate. Add any forms, campaigns and service tools you keep to the cost of your new CRM.</p><p>Sales users need the appropriate Sales Seats for Professional features. A cheaper Core Seat does not give a seller the same access.</p></div></div>
    <p class="lp-small">New-customer Starter offer: $7 per seat monthly with annual upfront payment or $10 monthly. Get the renewal price in writing. The standard rate is $20; the five-user table compares Professional.</p>
    <p class="lp-sources"><strong>Official HubSpot sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.hubspot.com/pricing/sales?currencyCode=USD" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sales Hub pricing ↗</a> · <a href="https://legal.hubspot.com/hubspot-product-and-services-catalog/products" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Product and seat limits ↗</a> · <a href="https://legal.hubspot.com/hubspot-product-and-services-catalog/add-ons" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Extra calling capacity ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/crm/starter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Starter offer ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/crm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Free CRM limits ↗</a></p>
    <div class="hs-decisions hs-after" id="stay-or-switch"><div><p class="lp-kicker">Keep it</p><h3>The current plan does the work.</h3><p>Your reps can manage deals, follow up and report properly. Avoid a migration just to save a small amount on seats.</p></div><div><p class="lp-kicker">Upgrade it</p><h3>The missing feature has clear value.</h3><p>Price Professional when the wider HubSpot setup saves real work across teams. Ask the vendor to demonstrate your process on the plan being quoted.</p></div><div><p class="lp-kicker">Replace it</p><h3>The required tier is too much.</h3><p>You mainly need a pipeline, calls and follow-up. Test two alternatives and compare the full first-year cost before committing.</p></div></div>
    <div class="lp-note"><strong>Our verdict: make the upgrade justify its cost.</strong><p>HubSpot can earn its price when the business uses the wider platform. A small team paying for basic sales work should compare the simpler options before moving up a tier.</p></div>
  </div></section>

